WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:04.250
JANOW: Well, good morning, everyone.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:06.500
Pleasure to see you here today.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:12.980
It's my great privilege to moderate this morning's
discussion with Ambassador Michael Froman.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:19.850
We'll have a brief conversation, and then
we'll open it up to hear your questions and

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:20.850
comments.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.740
I believe this discussion is on the record.

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:30.390
I think-Ambassador Froman is so well known
to all of us-I will be very brief in my introduction.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:39.620
But I noticed that he was sworn in as USTR
almost exactly three years ago today, so I-they

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.100
may be dog years, seven for one-
FROMAN: It seems like 21, yes.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.100
(Laughter.)

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.739
JANOW: -but it is a great accomplishment.

00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:56.140
As the U.S. trade representative, he is, of
course, responsible for all trade negotiations,

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:02.890
a spokesman on international trade, principal
interlocutor with Congress and with our major

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:04.030
trading partners.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:10.130
Before that he was, of course, in the White
House as assistant to the president and deputy

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:15.910
national security advisor for international
economic affairs, and coordinating on international

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:22.630
economic policy and trade, energy finance,
climate, and development and democracy issues,

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:25.540
so an enormous range of responsibilities.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:36.270
He was sherpa for the G-20/G-8, and had an
earlier period in government as well at Treasury

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.099
and in the National Security Council.

00:01:38.099 --> 00:01:45.090
So I can't imagine anyone more ready for this
enormous responsibility and playing such an

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.990
extraordinary role.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:53.890
Let me first invite you, if you would, to
help us as we're talking today about TPP and

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.890
U.S. leadership in Asia.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:02.500
Could you help us frame our understanding
of what you see as the key attributes of this

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.560
agreement and what it means for the United
States?

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:05.560
FROMAN: Sure.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.020
Well, first of all, thanks for having me.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.520
It's good to see a lot of familiar and generally
friendly faces.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.520
(Laughter.)

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.531
So good to-good to be back here at the Council.

00:02:15.531 --> 00:02:20.940
Look, the Trans-Pacific Partnership currently
is 12 countries representing about 40 percent

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.890
of the global economy.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:29.950
The agreement eliminates traditional trade
barriers, like tariffs-18,000 tariffs on American

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:34.670
exports; completely eliminates tariffs on
manufactured products; eliminates, or greatly

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:40.020
reduces, or opens up markets to a wide range
of agricultural trade as well.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.060
It's very important when it comes to services
in terms of opening up markets.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:48.680
We're locking them open so that-we have a
major services surplus from the United States.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.450
We're a globally competitive services provider,
and it opens that as well.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:58.590
But as important as those are is the rules
that are equally important-the fact that this-the

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:02.890
agreement raises labor and environmental standards
around the world to the highest level ever,

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:07.090
and these are fully enforceable standards;
that it's the first trade agreement to really

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:12.069
take on the digital economy, issues around
e-commerce and the free flow of data across

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:16.870
borders; pushing back on new forms of digital
protectionism, with countries trying to put

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:22.190
walls around the internet or require companies
to move their infrastructure to a country

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:23.990
in order to serve that country.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:29.530
It takes a major step forward in putting disciplines
on state-owned enterprises, so that when government-owned

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:34.000
corporations compete with our private firms,
they have to do so on a fair and level playing

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.430
field or we can impose sanctions on that country.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:42.710
And so it's very important because this region,
the Asia-Pacific region, it's currently about

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:47.460
a billion middle-class consumers, expected
to grow to 3 billion middle-class consumers

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:51.640
by 2030, some of the largest, fastest growing
economies in the world.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:53.990
We need to be part of those markets.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:58.160
And we want to make sure that the rules of
the road for that region reflect our interests

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.460
and our values, and that we're not ceding
that role to others, whether it's China or

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:02.460
others.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.490
And so that's why it's so important that we
get it done, and that we get it done this

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:06.490
year.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:07.490
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:12.569
I'm sure we'll turn in a few moments to the
question of getting it done, but could you

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:17.480
just say a little bit more about how we should
think about this agreement?

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:21.810
We have trade agreements-free trade agreements
with five of the TPP partners already.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:30.410
So, in a sense, how much further to we go
in this framework?

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:37.160
And do we think of it primarily as an economic
framework, or is it, in your mind, equally

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.960
important as a foreign policy or security
framework?

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.870
FROMAN: So, first of all, I think all trade
agreements first and foremost have to be justified

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:50.669
and defended on their economic grounds, what
they do to promote jobs and growth in the

00:04:50.669 --> 00:04:54.780
United States, and TPP holds up very well
under that analysis.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:05:00.860
The Peterson Institute has estimated it'll
add about $130 billion to GDP when implemented,

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:06.330
that it will increase U.S. exports by about
$350 billion a year.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.199
And the reason why that's important is that
we know that every billion dollars of exports

00:05:10.199 --> 00:05:16.781
supports somewhere between 5(,000) and 7,000
U.S. jobs, and those jobs pay up to 18 percent

00:05:16.781 --> 00:05:19.460
more on average than non-export related jobs.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:23.120
So if you're worried about wage stagnation,
you're worried about income inequality, the

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.160
majority of the benefits of this agreement
go to workers, both skilled and unskilled

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.830
workers, according to the Peterson Institute.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:32.610
And this is one way of making sure that we're
addressing those issues.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:36.750
It is equally important on a strategic level
and from a position of U.S. leadership.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:42.320
You know, I mentioned the rules in TPP, but
as we speak there are-countries are moving

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.690
ahead with their own trade agreements, their
own regional arrangements.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:52.169
And whether it's RCEP, which is the mega-regional
agreement that China's leading, it doesn't

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:56.960
include labor and environmental provisions,
it doesn't include disciplines on state-owned

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:01.560
enterprises, it doesn't commit to a free and
open internet.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:06.820
And so it's very important that, from a U.S.
leadership perspective, we're there helping-working

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:12.120
with our like-minded partners in the region
to set these rules of the road and not giving

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.250
that up.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:18.220
You know, also, you-people like Prime Minister
Lee, who has said, if you can't deal with

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:24.660
us on cars and services and agriculture, can
we depend on you when it comes to security

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.380
and military matters?

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:28.490
And I think that's a very important sentiment.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:33.770
These countries very much want to be embedded
with us, or want us embedded with them in

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:39.220
the region, both for economic purposes-so
that they can diversify their partnerships,

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:43.290
diversify their markets-but also, very importantly,
from a strategic perspective.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:47.730
And they see this partnership as having broader
political and strategic ramifications.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.440
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:57.790
So do you see the agreement as provoking an
economic reform agenda in TPP countries that

00:06:57.790 --> 00:06:59.669
is meaningful to the United States?

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:01.551
FROMAN: I think that's absolutely right.

00:07:01.551 --> 00:07:07.259
I mean, whether it's in Japan, where the-Prime
Minister Abe has viewed TPP as part of his

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:13.960
third arrow in Abenomics, the structural reform
agenda, and sees TPP as encouraging the kind

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:19.930
of reform of the agrarian sector that he has
been advocating for; or in Vietnam, where

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:25.210
the Vietnamese leadership has agreed to allow
independent unions for the first time, or

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:30.550
to reform or to use TPP to reform their state-owed
enterprise sector, or to raise their IP standards

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.480
because they want to become a more innovative
economy.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:39.490
So, from country to country, they've looked
at this as a way of helping to support a domestic

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:40.500
reform agenda.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.650
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:50.610
You know, I think the consequences of a loss
of American leadership is a bit of an ephemeral

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:55.210
concept to convince, you know, the electorate.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:08:00.460
And I wonder, how do you-how do you engage
that?

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:08.590
How do you think the consequences really are
if, in fact, this doesn't go forward, and

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:12.300
the U.S. has negotiated this, and countries
have made commitments in good faith?

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:13.300
What are the consequences?

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:16.410
FROMAN: Well, first, there are economic consequences.

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:21.741
Again, the Peterson Institute has estimated
that even just a one-year delay would be a

00:08:21.741 --> 00:08:25.860
permanent cost to the U.S. economy of $94
billion.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.570
That's about a $700 tax on every American
household.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:32.349
Then there's the cost to U.S. leadership.

00:08:32.349 --> 00:08:39.240
Again, whether-you know, we have been the
primary driver of a rules-based, international,

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.669
open system since the Second World War.

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:47.679
It's allowed countries like Japan and throughout
Europe to rebuild themselves.

00:08:47.679 --> 00:08:51.120
It's allowed developing countries to become
emerging economies.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.889
It's lifted-it's help lift hundreds of millions
of people out of poverty.

00:08:55.889 --> 00:08:58.610
And that system is something we can't take
for granted.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:02.880
We shouldn't be complacent about it because
there are other alternatives being promoted

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:05.490
out there-more statist, more mercantilist
systems.

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:09.759
And we think it's very important that the
rules-based system, where every country has

00:09:09.759 --> 00:09:13.999
certain rights, where there's dispute settlement
that they can count on, where it's not just

00:09:13.999 --> 00:09:18.809
big countries pushing little ones around,
that that's a very important part of a stable

00:09:18.809 --> 00:09:23.699
and prosperous Asia-Pacific region, and more
generally an international system.

00:09:23.699 --> 00:09:26.779
And then, thirdly, there are the strategic
costs.

00:09:26.779 --> 00:09:31.290
Prime Minister Key of New Zealand has said,
you know, the rest of the world isn't going

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:32.800
to just stand by.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:38.029
Beijing will fill whatever void we create,
and other countries will feel the need to

00:09:38.029 --> 00:09:40.130
line up accordingly.

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:42.920
And that cannot be in the interest of the
United States.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:47.800
And so, as people are critical of TPP-I mean,
it's been interesting.

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:52.449
There's been a lot of criticism of China in
the-in the recent debate, and I think a lot

00:09:52.449 --> 00:09:54.139
of that criticism is well-founded.

00:09:54.139 --> 00:10:00.220
And it's something that we need to constantly
press China on: to live up to a rules-based

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:04.379
system, implement their obligations, and be
prepared to take enforcement actions where

00:10:04.379 --> 00:10:05.379
they-where they don't.

00:10:05.379 --> 00:10:11.720
But TPP is one of the greatest tools we have
for raising the standards in this region.

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:15.410
And as other countries raise the standards,
it'll force China to up its game as well in

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:16.410
order to compete.

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:18.269
And that's in all of our interests.

00:10:18.269 --> 00:10:22.439
So it doesn't make a lot of sense to me to
be critical of China on one hand and then

00:10:22.439 --> 00:10:28.709
be willing to give up one of our most important
tools for creating the environment around

00:10:28.709 --> 00:10:31.759
China that will be important to raise China's
performance as well.

00:10:31.759 --> 00:10:32.759
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:10:32.759 --> 00:10:38.890
So do you see this as triggering China into
altering its own practices?

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:46.350
Or do you think it accentuates this argument
in China that the U.S. is trying to contain

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:47.350
China?

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:51.259
FROMAN: We've been very open with the Chinese
from the start of this negotiation.

00:10:51.259 --> 00:10:53.070
This isn't directed against them.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:54.709
It's not an effort to contain them.

00:10:54.709 --> 00:11:00.259
It is an effort to raise standards in the
region and around the world.

00:11:00.259 --> 00:11:04.019
And the Chinese have followed the negotiations
in great detail.

00:11:04.019 --> 00:11:08.889
We've kept them very much up to date throughout
the negotiations.

00:11:08.889 --> 00:11:14.779
And I think, ultimately, again, because as
countries like Vietnam and Malaysia and the

00:11:14.779 --> 00:11:17.319
other countries who have now signaled they'd
like to join TPP in the future.

00:11:17.319 --> 00:11:19.040
You know, we have 12 countries in the agreement.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.959
You know, we've heard from another 12 or 13
economies that they would like to join TPP

00:11:24.959 --> 00:11:28.749
in the future, or they'd like to have conversations
about joining TPP in the future.

00:11:28.749 --> 00:11:34.339
As this becomes the set of rules that defines
what a high-standard economy is all about,

00:11:34.339 --> 00:11:38.619
I think it's going to help encourage a competitive
race to the top.

00:11:38.619 --> 00:11:40.649
And that's good-I think, again, good for all
of us.

00:11:40.649 --> 00:11:41.679
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:11:41.679 --> 00:11:43.930
Let's turn to the United States.

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:54.029
And obviously, trade has, you know, been negatively
cast in the election process by both of the

00:11:54.029 --> 00:11:56.470
candidates thus far.

00:11:56.470 --> 00:12:02.399
So what is your strategy for advancing this
agreement in this very difficult political

00:12:02.399 --> 00:12:03.399
context?

00:12:03.399 --> 00:12:07.119
And how do you appeal to the American public
to recognize the values?

00:12:07.119 --> 00:12:14.119
FROMAN: Well, first, I think the good news
is that, as-now that the agreement is done,

00:12:14.119 --> 00:12:18.569
it's been out there in public since November,
people have had a chance to dig into it, they're

00:12:18.569 --> 00:12:23.499
seeing the value that's in there, what's in
it for different constituencies or different

00:12:23.499 --> 00:12:24.499
stakeholders.

00:12:24.499 --> 00:12:26.470
And those stakeholders are beginning to speak
out.

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:33.749
So you have broad support across virtually
the whole economy, whether it's the agriculture

00:12:33.749 --> 00:12:38.079
sector; the manufacturing sector; the high-tech
sector; the business sector, broadly; the

00:12:38.079 --> 00:12:42.759
small-business sector, very importantly, because
we have a real focus in the agreement on helping

00:12:42.759 --> 00:12:46.769
small businesses engage in global trade.

00:12:46.769 --> 00:12:50.910
And that support is beginning to trickle through
to members of Congress, as well.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:56.060
I'm spending a lot of my time, when I'm not
traveling abroad, up on the Hill talking to

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:57.060
members of Congress.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:01.079
And, again, the good news is, as they dig
into the details and they see what's in it

00:13:01.079 --> 00:13:06.600
for their constituents, which is their primary
driving-the driving force behind their decision-making,

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:13.720
they are seeing both the potential-the positive
upside-and what's at risk in not getting it

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:14.720
done.

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:20.130
You know, just to give you-to give you one
example, the cattlemen, the beef-the beef

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:21.130
producers.

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:27.689
Because Australia already has an FTA with
Japan, and so it faces a lower tariff exporting

00:13:27.689 --> 00:13:31.360
beef into Japan than we do, we're losing market
share.

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:34.670
And that's going to continue until TPP gets
put into place.

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:39.680
So we're losing over $120 million a year of
beef exports to Japan.

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:44.649
As constituents really dig into that and say
there's a lot in here for us in getting this

00:13:44.649 --> 00:13:50.420
done and there's a lot to lose if this gets
delayed, that-those voices on Capitol Hill

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:51.420
are being heard.

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:54.410
And I think, at the end of the day, there
will be the necessary support to get it done.

00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:55.429
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:13:55.429 --> 00:14:04.420
So, you know, it seems to me that, ever since
NAFTA, administrations have had to justify

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:11.109
trade agreements, you know, state by state
and sector by sector, stitching together the

00:14:11.109 --> 00:14:12.910
necessary coalitions.

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:15.220
Where is the heart of your coalition around
this, do you think?

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:17.259
Or is it really very broad-based?

00:14:17.259 --> 00:14:19.410
FROMAN: Well, it's pretty-it's pretty broad-based.

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:25.619
There are regional elements to it, but again,
I think the-one of the things I'm most proud

00:14:25.619 --> 00:14:29.670
of is that we worked very hard not to leave
any sector behind.

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:34.620
So we have the support of the textile producers
in the United States-this is the first time

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:39.041
they've really been unified behind a trade
agreement-and the apparel importers, the people

00:14:39.041 --> 00:14:40.869
who rely on imported clothing.

00:14:40.869 --> 00:14:46.490
We have the support of the domestic footwear
producers and the footwear importers.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:52.579
We have the broad support of the agricultural
community, with very few exceptions, of the

00:14:52.579 --> 00:14:55.059
manufacturers, of the service providers.

00:14:55.059 --> 00:15:00.040
The technology companies have now come out,
the internet association, the apps developers,

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:01.480
Google, et cetera.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.429
And all of that has been very, very positive.

00:15:03.429 --> 00:15:09.149
And so, again, as members of Congress are
hearing about that and beginning to get a

00:15:09.149 --> 00:15:14.239
feel for what's really at stake, there-I think
the necessary support will be there.

00:15:14.239 --> 00:15:15.239
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:15:15.239 --> 00:15:17.999
And could you speak about where you see the
opposition?

00:15:17.999 --> 00:15:24.410
You know, I've heard, of course, sectoral
opposition-you know, concern by biologics,

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:32.559
you know, Senator Hatch; concern about investor-state,
which is a bigger, more systemic kind of concern;

00:15:32.559 --> 00:15:40.040
and, of course, by labor, although you have
ambitious labor provisions in here.

00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:42.389
So could you speak about the opposition?

00:15:42.389 --> 00:15:43.389
FROMAN: Sure.

00:15:43.389 --> 00:15:48.470
There are a handful of issues that Congress
identified when the agreement was done that

00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:52.540
they wanted us to make sure we were fully
addressing.

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:56.809
And, bit by bit, we've been able to address
those.

00:15:56.809 --> 00:16:02.809
So there were issues around pork and issues
around dairy, and those groups are now fully

00:16:02.809 --> 00:16:03.809
supportive.

00:16:03.809 --> 00:16:07.869
There were issues around the financial services
sector and the free flow of data, and I think

00:16:07.869 --> 00:16:10.949
we're quite close to reaching a resolution
of that issue.

00:16:10.949 --> 00:16:15.779
I think the main outstanding issue right now
has to do-and you mentioned biologics, which

00:16:15.779 --> 00:16:18.990
is a(n) intellectual property rights issue.

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:24.959
And we are working with members of Congress
and with the industry to try and find solutions

00:16:24.959 --> 00:16:32.149
there to reassure them about the issues that
they care about without reopening the agreement

00:16:32.149 --> 00:16:34.759
or trying to renegotiate the agreement.

00:16:34.759 --> 00:16:39.139
You know, I think in terms of the opposition,
as you mentioned, there are a lot of things

00:16:39.139 --> 00:16:43.689
in here that are very strong on labor, the
strongest labor provisions ever.

00:16:43.689 --> 00:16:49.850
The Vietnamese-if you look at what the Vietnamese
have agreed to in terms of allowing independent

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:56.040
unions that can elect their own leaders, control
their own finances, conduct strikes, affiliate

00:16:56.040 --> 00:17:03.249
with whom they choose, again, assistance from
outside from the AFL or the ILO or others,

00:17:03.249 --> 00:17:05.429
it's really quite groundbreaking.

00:17:05.429 --> 00:17:11.030
And some of the other labor measures that
countries have taken on on their own to address

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:13.500
outstanding issues have been really quite
groundbreaking.

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:18.800
But it is true that organized labor is strongly
and unified against it.

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:19.910
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:17:19.910 --> 00:17:25.880
Could you speak a moment about the strategy-legislative
strategy for advancing this forward?

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:31.230
I mean, the expectation is that this is-that
you're pursuing a lame-duck strategy.

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:40.390
And, you know, I would just observe that the
president of the United States always ultimately

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:46.710
has to really weigh in and make it a priority
to get over the finish line.

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:49.860
What do you see in the six months ahead?

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:54.820
FROMAN: Well, we'd like to get it done as
quickly as possible, as early as possible.

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:59.280
And we're working with congressional leaders
and with the leaders of our committees-the

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:05.210
Finance Committee and the Ways and Means Committee-to
chart that pathway forward-laying the groundwork,

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:09.690
doing the preparatory work, drafting the bills,
drafting the reports that need to get done-so

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:16.880
that, when that window of opportunity opens,
we'll be ready to walk through it.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:21.290
And that's what our-that's what the work over
the next few months is going to take.

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:24.840
And again, I think there's a lot of desire
to get it done.

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:28.820
There's a recognition that if it doesn't get
done this year, it's unclear when it would

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:32.080
get done and how long it would have to linger.

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:39.840
And that has serious costs, as we talked about,
both economically and politically and strategically.

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:44.630
And so our strategy is just to work with individual
members, make sure they know what's in the

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:47.860
agreement, that they appreciate what's in
it for their constituents or stakeholders

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:52.910
that they care about, and then move forward
when the time-when the time is right.

00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:54.010
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:18:54.010 --> 00:18:55.010
OK.

00:18:55.010 --> 00:18:57.790
FROMAN: And the president-and you mentioned
the president; president has been very much-he's

00:18:57.790 --> 00:18:58.790
fully invested in this.

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:05.000
We have a whole-of-White-House, whole-of-Cabinet
effort underway with hundreds of events around

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:08.890
the country by Cabinet, sub-Cabinet officials.

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:13.760
If you haven't seen it already, I commend
you the president's performance on Jimmy Fallon

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:20.000
last week where he slow-jammed the news with
a good plug for TPP right in the middle of

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:21.000
it.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:24.110
So he's very much fully invested in this effort
as well.

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:25.110
JANOW: OK.

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:26.110
Well, that's a first, I think.

00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:27.670
FROMAN: That is a first.

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:28.810
(Laughter.)

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:33.340
JANOW: You know, one of the things you didn't
mention which I know has gotten some attention

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:40.950
and which has some controversy around it is
currency.

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:44.970
Could you speak to what-there is a currency
agreement of some sort.

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:49.240
I have not read it myself, I must admit.

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:53.110
But it doesn't apply sanctions, I believe.

00:19:53.110 --> 00:19:57.880
Does it take you forward in collaboration
in a new way?

00:19:57.880 --> 00:20:03.970
And, you know, this is really not an area
that trade has dealt with historically.

00:20:03.970 --> 00:20:06.180
FROMAN: I think-that's right.

00:20:06.180 --> 00:20:12.190
This is the first time we have an agreement
on currency among a group of trading partners

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:13.190
to an agreement.

00:20:13.190 --> 00:20:15.230
And it's-really, it's a three-part agreement.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:21.420
It lays out criteria of what appropriate exchange
rate policy is, drawing from the IMF and the

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:24.280
G-20 and the G-7.

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:30.270
It requires certain transparency so that for
the first time, we'll have visibility into

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:36.360
how central banks intervene in the market,
how often they intervene, when they intervene.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:39.900
And then it has an accountability mechanism
where the finance authorities get together

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:45.050
on a regular basis and assess each other's
performance against those criteria.

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:47.020
So it's the first time ever we've had something
like this.

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:48.430
I think it's a major step forward.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:53.880
And the Treasury Department negotiated this
with its counterparts around the TPP countries.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:59.300
What it doesn't do at the end of the day is
end with trade sanctions, which is what some

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:02.680
of the critics have advocated.

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:08.240
And the reason for that is, well, we may have
a particular view of what currency manipulation

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:13.690
looks like; other countries have-in our view
wrongly-but other countries have looked at

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:18.740
our monetary policy, quantitative easing,
and have said, that has the same effect on

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:20.470
relative currency values.

00:21:20.470 --> 00:21:25.900
And we would never subject our Federal Reserve
and our monetary policy to a trade tribunal

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:28.210
and trade sanctions, and no other country
would either.

00:21:28.210 --> 00:21:30.590
So it does stop short of that.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:35.100
But between this new agreement and some other
tools that Congress gave the Treasury Department

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:40.100
in the recently passed customs bill, I think
we have more teeth, the Treasury Department

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:44.940
has more teeth to deal with the issues of
currency manipulation than ever before, and

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:46.360
I think it is a significant step forward.

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:47.400
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:50.790
Let me ask you two other quick questions before
we open it up.

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:53.640
They're a little bit more sector-specific.

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:59.030
You've alluded to disciplines around state-owned
enterprises.

00:21:59.030 --> 00:22:01.780
And could you just say more about that?

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:07.270
Is that-some of those have dealt with transparency
and also procurement practices.

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:09.510
We have an international procurement agreement.

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:12.660
China is not yet a party to that.

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:17.390
So what is this framework doing that we don't
have at the multilateral or other level?

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:18.760
FROMAN: So this goes further.

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:26.650
It looks at both the purchases and sales of
products and services, so it goes beyond goods

00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:30.830
to cross-border services and says, if you're
a state-owned enterprise, you've got to-and

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:35.530
you engage in commercial activity, you have
to engage on a commercial basis.

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:40.960
And if you don't, and if the noncommercial
assistance that you receive from your government,

00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:47.630
whether it's subsidies or other measures,
has an adverse effect on a private firm, we

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:49.190
have a trade action against you.

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:55.460
And that adverse effect could be us trying
to sell something into Vietnam and a Vietnamese

00:22:55.460 --> 00:22:57.970
SOE distorting the market.

00:22:57.970 --> 00:23:04.750
It could be a Vietnamese SOE operating in
the United States having a green field investment

00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:08.360
in the United States but undercutting our
private firms.

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:10.340
Or it could be in a third TPP country.

00:23:10.340 --> 00:23:11.680
And that's never been done before.

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:18.370
And so in any of those cases, if it has an
adverse effect on our private firms, then

00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:22.680
we can bring a trade action against the government
and ultimately impose sanctions if it doesn't

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:24.060
get rectified.

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:28.040
And we think that's important because-and
again, in a lot of the public debate we're

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:33.900
hearing about trade, there's an underlying
theme that people worry that the system isn't

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:39.370
fair or that other countries aren't playing
by the same rules that we play by.

00:23:39.370 --> 00:23:45.080
And this is one of those ways of rectifying
that by saying that a state-owned enterprise

00:23:45.080 --> 00:23:48.820
competing against a private firm has to play
by the same rules of fairness than anybody

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:52.250
else, and if they don't, we have an enforcement
action against their government.

00:23:52.250 --> 00:23:53.330
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:23:53.330 --> 00:23:54.330
Very good.

00:23:54.330 --> 00:24:01.590
Let me ask you a bit more about the digital
economy because more and more I think is trade,

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:07.470
whether it's services or manufactured goods,
is really implicated by the ability to have

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:10.210
data flow across borders.

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:15.480
If I'm understanding, this agreement is the
first that really is going to prohibit data

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:16.480
localization.

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:17.480
Is that fair?

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:18.480
FROMAN: That's right.

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:19.480
I think that's right.

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:20.480
I think that's right.

00:24:20.480 --> 00:24:24.040
Look, it's very important because as you mention,
first of all, this isn't just about tech firms

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:25.970
and their importance of having data across
borders.

00:24:25.970 --> 00:24:27.200
Data is part of everything.

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:28.380
It's part of services.

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:30.440
It's part of manufacturing.

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:36.330
And firms need to be able to move data from
one jurisdiction to another.

00:24:36.330 --> 00:24:40.650
And this is the first time that there is a
prohibition on data localization, prohibition

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:46.590
on forcing a country to have servers in every
market that they want to provide services

00:24:46.590 --> 00:24:52.190
in in order to be able to participate so they
can have their data stored where it's secure,

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:53.960
where there is integrity to the network.

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:59.210
There are a whole series of other provisions
about not imposing duties on digital products

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:05.770
like apps, and so making sure that there is
a free flow of innovation in that regard as

00:25:05.770 --> 00:25:06.770
well.

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:10.950
And then from a small business perspective-you
know, most small and medium-size businesses

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:15.570
don't have widespread distribution systems
or sales forces around the world.

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:17.330
They rely on e-commerce.

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:23.880
And when they're selling something from-on
Etsy in Brooklyn from here to somebody in

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:28.460
Australia-and I met with a number of Etsy
producers who were-who were doing just that-they're

00:25:28.460 --> 00:25:34.810
using telecom services, software services,
electronic payment services, express delivery

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:35.810
services.

00:25:35.810 --> 00:25:37.900
And all those services are covered in this
agreement.

00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:43.150
So we've worked very hard to make sure that
the rules are such that, again, support the

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:47.620
continued development of the digital economy,
including for e-commerce, especially for small

00:25:47.620 --> 00:25:52.090
and medium-size businesses so they can all
take advantage of the opening of the markets

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:53.940
that we've been able to secure.

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:56.000
JANOW: Thank you very much.

00:25:56.000 --> 00:26:01.170
With that, why don't I open it up for questions
and comments-questions, not comments-from

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:03.510
our very distinguished audience.

00:26:03.510 --> 00:26:14.452
And if you could just briefly identify yourself
and ask a brief question.

00:26:14.452 --> 00:26:15.452
Sir.

00:26:15.452 --> 00:26:16.452
Q: Good morning.

00:26:16.452 --> 00:26:17.452
Thank you.

00:26:17.452 --> 00:26:18.452
Ted Pulling from JPMorgan Asset Management.

00:26:18.452 --> 00:26:22.710
You mentioned the window of opportunity which
you need to get this passed before the election.

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:25.921
Can you tell us approximately when that window
of opportunity is?

00:26:25.921 --> 00:26:28.010
There is not a lot of time.

00:26:28.010 --> 00:26:34.630
FROMAN: Well, I think Leader McConnell of
the Senate has made clear publicly that he

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:38.850
doesn't want to see a vote before the election.

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:44.750
So that really means-from the Senate perspective,
anyway-in the lame-duck period.

00:26:44.750 --> 00:26:48.180
But to even do this in the lame duck, you
want to do as much of the preparatory work

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:51.920
as possible under trade promotion authority
beforehand, and that's what we're working

00:26:51.920 --> 00:26:53.240
on now.

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:56.970
JANOW: Other questions.

00:26:56.970 --> 00:26:58.220
Yes.

00:26:58.220 --> 00:27:02.630
Q: Tim Ferguson with Forbes.

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:08.410
So both major candidates are pledging to rework
the agreement in some way once they are elected,

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:10.990
once he or she is elected.

00:27:10.990 --> 00:27:16.640
What kind of wiggle room is there actually
to do that reworking without renegotiating,

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:19.250
if that is the event?

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:25.640
FROMAN: This is a much more complicated agreement
than I think anything we tried before.

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:32.830
You know, as Merit mentioned, half the countries
we already have FTAs with, and-so you're not

00:27:32.830 --> 00:27:38.150
trading market access for agreement to a set
of rules; you're just building support for

00:27:38.150 --> 00:27:40.370
those rules.

00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:44.240
This wasn't an agreement where eight or nine
countries agreed on everything and it was

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:47.990
just a process of pulling the other three
countries over the time.

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:52.620
This was an agreement where every single provision
had a different set of parties who were in

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:58.600
favor or against it and you had to build consensus
issue by issue.

00:27:58.600 --> 00:28:06.020
And as a result, it's a very-every country
feels like it found the right balance, and

00:28:06.020 --> 00:28:11.300
it has to justify that balance at home politically
as well.

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:16.110
And so pulling the thread on one issue I'm
very concerned will lead to the unraveling

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:18.060
across several other issues.

00:28:18.060 --> 00:28:24.000
So I do not think this is an agreement that
can be easily renegotiated or re-opened.

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:30.310
That doesn't mean that in the process of implementation-and
we have a robust process of making sure countries

00:28:30.310 --> 00:28:33.850
are meeting their obligations; we work with
Congress on that-that there aren't things

00:28:33.850 --> 00:28:39.720
that we can do to give reassurance that we're
addressing issues with the countries and with

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:40.980
Congress.

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:45.730
But I think opening up the Pandora's Box of
renegotiation I think will ultimately lead

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:47.480
to it unraveling itself.

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:49.590
JANOW: Professor Fox.

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:51.010
Q: Hello.

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:54.530
Eleanor Fox, New York University.

00:28:54.530 --> 00:29:01.940
I want to push further on Dean Janow's question
to you about opposition to free trade agreements.

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:07.110
And it's more than TPP, but it's focused on
TPP a lot right now.

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:14.230
You seem rather sanguine when you say people
should just look at the agreement and see

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:15.230
the benefits.

00:29:15.230 --> 00:29:16.450
And we've talked to all the stakeholders.

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:19.520
And all they have to do is look and see the
benefits.

00:29:19.520 --> 00:29:24.059
And yet, isn't there a sense that they have
looked-that is, for example, our candidates

00:29:24.059 --> 00:29:29.790
for president-that they have looked and they
don't see the benefits?

00:29:29.790 --> 00:29:30.790
Why is that?

00:29:30.790 --> 00:29:33.390
What is this gap of populist opposition?

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:41.240
Is it, in part, a concern that people lose
their jobs here and now, even though labor

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:43.760
wins, but particular people lose.

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:48.720
And perhaps they feel not enough is done to
compensate them?

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:54.490
I think, actually, it goes deeper and more
philosophically and would like to know what

00:29:54.490 --> 00:29:55.490
you think.

00:29:55.490 --> 00:30:01.840
But specifically, are the losers going to
be helped in some way that will satisfy them?

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:07.550
And then generally, is there yet a bigger
gap of just philosophical opposition to freer

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:13.850
trade on a thought and belief that our country
is worse off?

00:30:13.850 --> 00:30:18.059
FROMAN: So it's a very good question, and
a very timely one.

00:30:18.059 --> 00:30:24.620
If you ask most economists-well, if you look
at the economic situation in our country,

00:30:24.620 --> 00:30:29.900
we've seen a lot of improvement over the last
six or seven years in terms of unemployment

00:30:29.900 --> 00:30:36.900
has been cut in half, we've had, what is it,
75 months in a row of private sector job creation

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:39.270
and we're growing 2 and 3 percent.

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:42.350
We created 14 million new jobs.

00:30:42.350 --> 00:30:44.050
All that's very good.

00:30:44.050 --> 00:30:48.490
At the same time, we've seen wage stagnation
for the last 15 years or so.

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:50.070
This is only beginning to turn up.

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:53.690
I think it's gone up-wages have gone up about
2 1/2 percent last year.

00:30:53.690 --> 00:30:56.450
And they're on the same track again this year.

00:30:56.450 --> 00:30:58.880
We've seen widening income inequality.

00:30:58.880 --> 00:31:05.390
And I think those have contributed very significantly
to the populist feeling that you cite.

00:31:05.390 --> 00:31:09.720
Now, if you talk to most economists, they'll
tell you, if you look at the changing nature

00:31:09.720 --> 00:31:16.010
of the workforce, it's more automation than
globalization, but certainly globalization

00:31:16.010 --> 00:31:18.170
plays a role.

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:20.700
You don't get to vote on automation.

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:24.640
Nobody gets to vote on the next generation
or computers, or on the next generation of

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:27.250
robots that might affect the workforce.

00:31:27.250 --> 00:31:30.650
You don't really get to vote on globalization.

00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:32.270
Globalization is a force.

00:31:32.270 --> 00:31:38.240
It's the product of the containerization of
shipping, the spread of broadband, the integration

00:31:38.240 --> 00:31:41.980
of economies like China and Eastern Europe
that used to be closed to the world and are

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:44.590
now part of the global economy.

00:31:44.590 --> 00:31:46.520
You do get to vote on trade agreements.

00:31:46.520 --> 00:31:52.370
And so trade agreements become the proxy,
become the vessel into which people pour,

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:58.370
I think, their quite legitimate concerns about
the changing nature of the workforce and their

00:31:58.370 --> 00:32:01.120
concerns about wages and income inequality.

00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:07.001
Now, our perspective is while you don't get
to vote on globalization, trade agreements

00:32:07.001 --> 00:32:09.559
is now you shape globalization.

00:32:09.559 --> 00:32:12.720
Without trade agreements, we are an open economy
already.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:14.580
We have very little protection here.

00:32:14.580 --> 00:32:17.990
Our averaged applied tariff is 1.4 percent.

00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:21.680
We don't use regulations as a disguised barrier
to trade.

00:32:21.680 --> 00:32:25.690
So when we grow, we bring in imports from
all over the world.

00:32:25.690 --> 00:32:30.720
We compete right now with low wage countries
around the world.

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:35.240
With trade agreements, we have the opportunity
of removing barriers to those markets so that

00:32:35.240 --> 00:32:37.190
we can increase our exports.

00:32:37.190 --> 00:32:40.690
And we have the opportunity to raise standards
in those countries so there's a more level

00:32:40.690 --> 00:32:42.080
playing field.

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:45.180
And that's really what's been driving behind
the TPP.

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:48.730
It's a recognition that we do live in a global
economy, that we can't put that genie back

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:49.900
in the bottle.

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:53.920
But through trade agreements, we can take
action that reflects our interests, reflects

00:32:53.920 --> 00:33:00.020
our values, and shape the global economy in
a way that's more to the benefit of our workers.

00:33:00.020 --> 00:33:03.930
JANOW: May I ask one follow-up question?

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:10.610
You know, in other periods of fashioning a
coalition around international trade, you

00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:18.620
know, the administration arguably had more
tools than you do at this point in an administration,

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:25.400
in the current environment, whether it was
trade adjustment assistance or other kinds

00:33:25.400 --> 00:33:37.040
of tools to kind of buttress support or benefit
even constituencies in certain states or whatever

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:39.990
to reach the political compromises necessary.

00:33:39.990 --> 00:33:41.679
Do you have such tools?

00:33:41.679 --> 00:33:46.179
FROMAN: Well, when we secured trade promotion
authority last year from Congress, we were

00:33:46.179 --> 00:33:48.510
actually able to get a renewal of trade adjustment
assistance.

00:33:48.510 --> 00:33:50.820
I'm sorry I didn't answer that part of your
question.

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:53.860
So we had a six-year renewal of the trade
adjustment assistance program.

00:33:53.860 --> 00:33:59.170
It was expanded to cover not just manufacturing
workers but service workers, and not just

00:33:59.170 --> 00:34:03.390
workers who might be displaced by a trade
agreement but workers displaced by anything

00:34:03.390 --> 00:34:04.670
happening in the global economy.

00:34:04.670 --> 00:34:09.069
So whether it's coming from China or India,
where we don't have trade agreements, or whether

00:34:09.069 --> 00:34:12.440
it's coming from an FTA partner.

00:34:12.440 --> 00:34:17.089
But to the professor's point, there's always
more than can and should be done.

00:34:17.089 --> 00:34:23.519
And whether it's investing in education, and
skills building, and infrastructure, and all

00:34:23.519 --> 00:34:28.510
the things that we know are important to building
a highly productive economy here, there is

00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:29.659
more-there is more to be done.

00:34:29.659 --> 00:34:32.259
It's something that we've always advocated
for.

00:34:32.259 --> 00:34:35.409
And I think it's something that's getting
a lot of attention, properly, in the current

00:34:35.409 --> 00:34:36.409
debate.

00:34:36.409 --> 00:34:37.639
JANOW: Thank you.

00:34:37.639 --> 00:34:38.639
Sir.

00:34:38.639 --> 00:34:39.879
Q: Thank you.

00:34:39.879 --> 00:34:42.669
Earl Carr, representing Momentum Advisors.

00:34:42.669 --> 00:34:46.210
I was at a meeting with one of the key negotiators
for TPP.

00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:52.619
And she mentioned that Malaysia and Vietnam
would be best positioned to gain the most

00:34:52.619 --> 00:34:55.730
economic value from this agreement.

00:34:55.730 --> 00:34:56.849
Would you agree with that assessment?

00:34:56.849 --> 00:35:03.280
FROMAN: I think the Peterson Institute was
probably the most in-depth report on TPP,

00:35:03.280 --> 00:35:09.060
says on a percentage base-as a percentage
of their economy in terms of GDP growth or

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:14.059
growth in exports, those two economies are
likely to benefit the most.

00:35:14.059 --> 00:35:17.320
We benefit the most in absolute terms, but
of course we're the largest economy in the

00:35:17.320 --> 00:35:18.420
world.

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:21.730
They benefit the most in percentage terms.

00:35:21.730 --> 00:35:26.190
It's not particularly surprising, because
they countries that benefit the most are those

00:35:26.190 --> 00:35:27.960
who are eliminating the most barriers.

00:35:27.960 --> 00:35:32.319
They're the ones that are most protected now
and will be having to liberalize their economy,

00:35:32.319 --> 00:35:35.220
and they'll get the greatest gains from that
liberalization.

00:35:35.220 --> 00:35:37.849
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:35:37.849 --> 00:35:39.599
Professor Feldstein.

00:35:39.599 --> 00:35:41.349
Q: Hi.

00:35:41.349 --> 00:35:48.700
I thought one of the most significant things
you said is there really isn't scope for renegotiation

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:49.710
of the agreement.

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:56.599
So whatever the agreement is has to be voted
eventually up or down.

00:35:56.599 --> 00:36:05.240
Secretary Clinton has expressed her lack of
support for the agreement in its current form.

00:36:05.240 --> 00:36:13.279
So it sounds like you can't change the agreement
after she becomes elected, if she gets elected,

00:36:13.279 --> 00:36:14.849
in order to get her support.

00:36:14.849 --> 00:36:21.210
So does that mean that the strategy for getting
her to say, well, taken as a whole I like

00:36:21.210 --> 00:36:28.809
this deal, is in some of these collateral
things-changes in manpower programs or other

00:36:28.809 --> 00:36:32.510
things-since you can't change the basic agreement?

00:36:32.510 --> 00:36:37.900
FROMAN: Well, I won't comment on any particular
candidate, and I certainly can't speak for

00:36:37.900 --> 00:36:44.680
Secretary Clinton and what the perspective-what
her perspective might be going forward.

00:36:44.680 --> 00:36:49.450
What I can say is that this is the highest
standard agreement ever negotiated, again,

00:36:49.450 --> 00:36:55.470
across these various provisions; that I do
believe because of the complexities of the

00:36:55.470 --> 00:37:00.700
agreement, the number of countries, the number
of issues, the dynamics of the negotiation

00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:04.460
themselves that renegotiation's not an option.

00:37:04.460 --> 00:37:08.430
And by the way, these countries have their
own politics around these issues.

00:37:08.430 --> 00:37:12.569
And they're all going through their own ratification
procedures.

00:37:12.569 --> 00:37:19.730
And we need to take that into account as well
as they move forward, even as we're encouraging

00:37:19.730 --> 00:37:22.680
our Congress to do so as well.

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:28.240
I do think there are things that can be done
outside the agreement, as you mentioned, that

00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:34.650
will address the broader concerns about how
to make sure the American workforce is prepared

00:37:34.650 --> 00:37:42.039
to compete in the global economy and in the
TPP-in a TPP world.

00:37:42.039 --> 00:37:47.420
And I also believe that through the implementation
process we can work with Congress, with stakeholders,

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:53.130
with our trading partners to make sure that,
as we're interpreting elements of the agreement,

00:37:53.130 --> 00:37:57.880
as countries are bringing their systems into
compliance with the agreement, that we're

00:37:57.880 --> 00:38:01.230
doing everything necessary to ensure that
those issues are addressed.

00:38:01.230 --> 00:38:02.640
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:04.989
In the far back, please.

00:38:04.989 --> 00:38:05.989
Q: Hi.

00:38:05.989 --> 00:38:07.769
Mario-
JANOW: Is the microphone-

00:38:07.769 --> 00:38:08.769
Q: Oh, thank you.

00:38:08.769 --> 00:38:09.769
Sorry.

00:38:09.769 --> 00:38:11.380
I'm Mario Calvo-Platero, Sole 24 Ore.

00:38:11.380 --> 00:38:18.960
I just wanted to hear whether there is any
progress on TTIP, on the transatlantic agreement,

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:24.970
and whether there is a chance to do something
before the administration will go out.

00:38:24.970 --> 00:38:25.970
Thank you.

00:38:25.970 --> 00:38:29.349
FROMAN: There has been progress-actually,
quite a bit of progress-over the last eight

00:38:29.349 --> 00:38:30.970
months, I would say.

00:38:30.970 --> 00:38:32.589
And there is a chance to get it done this
year.

00:38:32.589 --> 00:38:36.369
And that is our objective, is to try-it won't
go through Congress, but to get it agreed

00:38:36.369 --> 00:38:40.599
to over the course of this administration.

00:38:40.599 --> 00:38:46.410
To do that, it's going to require continued
progress along the same kind of accelerated

00:38:46.410 --> 00:38:49.660
pace that we've seen over the last few months.

00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:56.059
It's also going to require some creative and
pragmatic and non-ideological approaches to

00:38:56.059 --> 00:39:02.140
resolving what inevitably are the hardest
outstanding issues.

00:39:02.140 --> 00:39:03.380
We've got the political will.

00:39:03.380 --> 00:39:07.680
And the president, when he was in Hanover
a couple of months ago, and then again at

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:13.249
the G-7, made very clear that the U.S. is
fully committed to doing everything we can

00:39:13.249 --> 00:39:17.819
do to try and reach an agreement this year.

00:39:17.819 --> 00:39:21.440
We hope that the Europeans are similarly focused.

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:28.390
They have a lot on their plate at the moment,
and a lot of concerns and countervailing forces,

00:39:28.390 --> 00:39:34.289
whether it's Brexit vote this week, or the
migrant crisis, or the hangover of the Greek

00:39:34.289 --> 00:39:42.900
crisis, or the rise of populism, or the spread
of referenda, or the euro-skepticism that

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:46.920
are very much affecting their day-to-day lives.

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:50.950
We hope that they've got the necessary focus
to get-and the political will to get this

00:39:50.950 --> 00:39:52.029
done as well.

00:39:52.029 --> 00:39:53.380
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:39:53.380 --> 00:39:55.630
In the front, David Sandalow.

00:39:55.630 --> 00:39:56.630
Q: Hi.

00:39:56.630 --> 00:39:57.630
David Sandalow.

00:39:57.630 --> 00:39:59.680
Columbia School of International and Public
Affairs.

00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:03.680
Assume that TPP does enter into force, how
do you see China relating to the agreement

00:40:03.680 --> 00:40:05.250
over time?

00:40:05.250 --> 00:40:09.809
FROMAN: Well, our view has always been this
is not directed against China.

00:40:09.809 --> 00:40:11.690
It's not about containing China.

00:40:11.690 --> 00:40:13.420
It's about setting high standards.

00:40:13.420 --> 00:40:19.519
But as other countries adopt these high standard-whether
it's Vietnam, Malaysia, or others, I think

00:40:19.519 --> 00:40:23.539
I mentioned that there's another dozen-or-so
countries who have indicated they'd like to

00:40:23.539 --> 00:40:28.940
potentially join TPP as well-it's going to
require China to up its game as well to be

00:40:28.940 --> 00:40:29.940
competitive.

00:40:29.940 --> 00:40:34.950
You know, when neighboring countries are offering
better intellectual property rights protection,

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:40.500
protection against trade secret theft, real
dispute settlement-recourse to real dispute

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:47.210
settlement, new stable labor and environmental
environments to operate in, that's going to

00:40:47.210 --> 00:40:50.990
require a race to-that's going to lead to
a race to the top for the region.

00:40:50.990 --> 00:40:52.019
That's our goal.

00:40:52.019 --> 00:40:54.660
And that will require China to up its game
as well.

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:56.970
And I think that's all-that's in all of our
interests.

00:40:56.970 --> 00:41:00.030
JANOW: One follow-up question about China.

00:41:00.030 --> 00:41:07.930
I think the administration has now had something
like 23 or 24 rounds of negotiations on the

00:41:07.930 --> 00:41:11.329
investment-bilateral investment agreement.

00:41:11.329 --> 00:41:20.609
And I wonder where you think that stands and
how it intersects with a broader trade agreement.

00:41:20.609 --> 00:41:25.519
Some argue, you know, that it can be viewed
as a stepping stone.

00:41:25.519 --> 00:41:27.950
Is that your view or do you think that's a
Chinese view?

00:41:27.950 --> 00:41:32.839
Or do you think on its own is what's the driver
of its completion?

00:41:32.839 --> 00:41:39.650
FROMAN: Well, I think a high-standard bilateral
investment treaty would require some very

00:41:39.650 --> 00:41:43.750
significant reforms of the Chinese economic
system.

00:41:43.750 --> 00:41:46.410
And that-we're having that dialogue with them.

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:52.880
Just last week they were in Washington again,
and gave us a revised-let's call it a negative

00:41:52.880 --> 00:41:58.450
list, which is the list of sectors where they're
not committing to openness.

00:41:58.450 --> 00:42:05.380
So we want to keep that list as narrow and
as short as possible.

00:42:05.380 --> 00:42:08.289
And we're in the process of assessing that
proposal.

00:42:08.289 --> 00:42:16.440
And we'll be further engaging with them actively
over the course of the summer and into the

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:17.440
fall.

00:42:17.440 --> 00:42:22.869
I think if we can achieve a high-standard
agreement you're moving from a world in which

00:42:22.869 --> 00:42:28.220
everything is prohibited unless it's expressly
approved to one where everything's approved

00:42:28.220 --> 00:42:31.220
unless expressly regulated or prohibited.

00:42:31.220 --> 00:42:35.630
And that's a significant change in terms of
getting the government out of the business

00:42:35.630 --> 00:42:39.229
of managing the economy.

00:42:39.229 --> 00:42:46.890
I think separate from TPP it really is a data
point or indication of China's commitment

00:42:46.890 --> 00:42:47.960
to reform.

00:42:47.960 --> 00:42:52.400
And that's the kind of dialogue we're having
with them, is how far they're willing to go

00:42:52.400 --> 00:42:58.779
to demonstrate that they are serious about
allowing market forces to play a dispositive

00:42:58.779 --> 00:43:01.519
effect on decision making in their system.

00:43:01.519 --> 00:43:02.519
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:43:02.519 --> 00:43:11.529
Do you see that agreement as offering some
protections or a better context for Chinese

00:43:11.529 --> 00:43:14.529
investment in the U.S., from a U.S. perspective?

00:43:14.529 --> 00:43:16.359
FROMAN: Oh, it's a bilateral agreement.

00:43:16.359 --> 00:43:20.280
We already have a very open investment regime.

00:43:20.280 --> 00:43:27.660
We don't have any-very few kinds of restrictions
that we face in other countries.

00:43:27.660 --> 00:43:31.380
And obviously in China we face enormous numbers
of restrictions.

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:37.349
You know, our CFIUS procedure, the Committee
on Foreign Investment in the United States,

00:43:37.349 --> 00:43:45.079
reviews mergers and acquisitions from a very
specific national security perspective.

00:43:45.079 --> 00:43:50.540
So we already-China investment in the U.S.
has grown dramatically over the last several

00:43:50.540 --> 00:43:51.540
years.

00:43:51.540 --> 00:43:57.180
They've had little problem investing in assets
here.

00:43:57.180 --> 00:44:00.920
And I think the BIT will help institutionalize
that.

00:44:00.920 --> 00:44:03.640
But we're already a very open economy when
it comes to investment.

00:44:03.640 --> 00:44:07.039
And we encourage investment from foreign investors
in the United States.

00:44:07.039 --> 00:44:08.039
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:44:08.039 --> 00:44:14.539
I was thinking, though, in terms of offering
a vehicle for structured conversations around

00:44:14.539 --> 00:44:20.210
problems when they arise, whether they're
perceived as arising in China or arising here,

00:44:20.210 --> 00:44:23.940
and whether the BIT is really a vehicle that
helps you get there.

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:26.029
FROMAN: We have a series of vehicles.

00:44:26.029 --> 00:44:30.990
And we just came back from the Strategic and
Economic Dialogue in Beijing a couple weeks

00:44:30.990 --> 00:44:31.990
ago.

00:44:31.990 --> 00:44:33.640
And investment issues were on that agenda.

00:44:33.640 --> 00:44:39.490
And we always share perspectives, our concerns
about problems that our investors are having

00:44:39.490 --> 00:44:43.289
in their market, and they raise concerns about
our market as well.

00:44:43.289 --> 00:44:47.339
And so I think it is important that we continue
to have that dialogue and address those issues.

00:44:47.339 --> 00:44:48.660
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:44:48.660 --> 00:44:49.660
Thank you.

00:44:49.660 --> 00:44:50.859
Over here, please.

00:44:50.859 --> 00:44:53.170
Q: My name is Masazumi Nakayama, Citigroup.

00:44:53.170 --> 00:44:56.029
I have a question about ISDS growth.

00:44:56.029 --> 00:45:03.430
And some countries' public opinion expressed
a fear of ISDS growth because a country thinks

00:45:03.430 --> 00:45:07.979
the U.S. always wins in the international
dispute.

00:45:07.979 --> 00:45:13.230
How can we help them feel more comfortable
about ISDS growth?

00:45:13.230 --> 00:45:18.619
FROMAN: Well, first, I think-ISDS is investor-state
dispute settlement.

00:45:18.619 --> 00:45:24.450
It's the mechanism of mutual international
arbitration where investors are able to protect

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:31.410
themselves from expropriation or other discriminatory
actions in a foreign market.

00:45:31.410 --> 00:45:36.619
Our perspective on it, first, is we provide
under our Constitution, the fifth amendment,

00:45:36.619 --> 00:45:42.680
the Takings Clause protection to all domestic
and foreign investors against expropriation

00:45:42.680 --> 00:45:44.430
without compensation.

00:45:44.430 --> 00:45:49.039
And what ISDS is really about is making sure
that our investors, when they're operating

00:45:49.039 --> 00:45:55.869
abroad, have a similar level of basic protection.

00:45:55.869 --> 00:46:00.299
But we share a number of the concerns that
have been raised about ISDS over the years.

00:46:00.299 --> 00:46:06.319
And in fact, since 2000, in each of our subsequent
agreements we have been working to reform

00:46:06.319 --> 00:46:13.559
ISDS, whether it's raising the standards and
tightening them up, adding various procedural

00:46:13.559 --> 00:46:18.609
and substantive safeguards, increasing transparency
of the proceedings, closing loopholes that

00:46:18.609 --> 00:46:23.069
we found in other countries' agreements, all
of those have been part of our agenda really

00:46:23.069 --> 00:46:27.140
for the last 15, 16 years.

00:46:27.140 --> 00:46:32.829
In the 30-plus years we've had agreements
with ISDS, and we have 51 agreements to which

00:46:32.829 --> 00:46:40.759
we're a party with ISDS, we've had a sum total
of 17 cases brought against the United States-13

00:46:40.759 --> 00:46:44.430
cases to conclusion, and the government has
never lost a case.

00:46:44.430 --> 00:46:47.710
We've had no cases brought in the last six
years.

00:46:47.710 --> 00:46:54.489
And so while ISDS use generally may be going
up around the world, we're not seeing a big

00:46:54.489 --> 00:47:00.809
influx of cases in the United States against
U.S. government action or U.S. regulation.

00:47:00.809 --> 00:47:05.750
And I think that's, in part, because we focus
so much in our agreements on raising the standards

00:47:05.750 --> 00:47:09.339
and adding these safeguards, and closing these
loopholes.

00:47:09.339 --> 00:47:14.031
Whereas, you know, there are 3,200 agreements
around the world that have ISDS, and they

00:47:14.031 --> 00:47:15.750
have varying standards.

00:47:15.750 --> 00:47:21.130
Our approach to TPP has been let's use TPP,
again, as a way of raising the standards for

00:47:21.130 --> 00:47:22.130
ISDS.

00:47:22.130 --> 00:47:25.559
And if TPP doesn't move forward, then you're
stuck with the existing standards.

00:47:25.559 --> 00:47:30.119
So if you don't like the current agreements
with ISDS, you should want to move forward

00:47:30.119 --> 00:47:33.829
with TPP because it actually tightens it and
reforms it further.

00:47:33.829 --> 00:47:37.539
But little things, like-lots of little things.

00:47:37.539 --> 00:47:43.190
An investor's expectations of profits alone
cannot be a basis of a suit.

00:47:43.190 --> 00:47:45.730
That's a reform in TPP.

00:47:45.730 --> 00:47:51.049
An investor's expectations that regulations
won't change, alone, cannot be the basis of

00:47:51.049 --> 00:47:52.049
a suit.

00:47:52.049 --> 00:47:53.470
The governments have a right to regulate.

00:47:53.470 --> 00:47:55.059
That's in TPP.

00:47:55.059 --> 00:47:58.489
Those are the kinds of reforms that we've
been able to negotiate with our trading partners

00:47:58.489 --> 00:48:06.690
that we think overall makes ISDS a mechanism
that appropriately protects investors while

00:48:06.690 --> 00:48:12.160
ensuring that governments can take the actions
they need to take in a bona fide way for regulation.

00:48:12.160 --> 00:48:16.930
JANOW: I, myself, have arbitrated a number
of these, as you know.

00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:23.440
And at least under NAFTA, you know, they've
been open hearings where, you know, anyone

00:48:23.440 --> 00:48:25.339
can show up.

00:48:25.339 --> 00:48:33.039
And I think some allow for amicus curiae submissions
by interested parties and so on.

00:48:33.039 --> 00:48:37.390
So, you know, they're not the kind of secret
forum that is often said.

00:48:37.390 --> 00:48:39.759
FROMAN: In TPP, we've agreed to webcast them.

00:48:39.759 --> 00:48:41.910
JANOW: Yeah, that was-
FROMAN: That makes them more transparent than

00:48:41.910 --> 00:48:42.910
the U.S. Supreme Court.

00:48:42.910 --> 00:48:44.950
JANOW: Mmm hmm, yeah.

00:48:44.950 --> 00:48:51.000
Well, there are reasons as a judge I would
say why a webcast is not a wonderful-(laughter).

00:48:51.000 --> 00:48:52.740
Yes, please.

00:48:52.740 --> 00:48:57.079
Q: Leila Heckman from Lebenthal.

00:48:57.079 --> 00:49:05.170
The candidate Hillary says she's going to-she
would renegotiate TPP.

00:49:05.170 --> 00:49:09.920
What aspects of TPP do you think she's referring
to?

00:49:09.920 --> 00:49:15.229
FROMAN: Again, I can't speak for her.

00:49:15.229 --> 00:49:19.339
I think she has flagged a couple of issues
in particular, including the currency issue

00:49:19.339 --> 00:49:26.809
that Merit mentioned, including ISDS, although
it's unclear what part of it she'd want to

00:49:26.809 --> 00:49:28.819
renegotiate.

00:49:28.819 --> 00:49:31.219
She has cited pharmaceutical provisions.

00:49:31.219 --> 00:49:41.020
She feels we did too much in the agreement
to protect pharmaceuticals.

00:49:41.020 --> 00:49:42.779
And she's flagged the rules of origin issue.

00:49:42.779 --> 00:49:44.680
Those are the four that I'm aware of.

00:49:44.680 --> 00:49:48.250
JANOW: Can I just ask a political question
further?

00:49:48.250 --> 00:49:53.309
You know, first term of the Obama administration,
obviously you're dealing with the global financial

00:49:53.309 --> 00:49:58.130
crisis, domestic issues; trade was not that
visible.

00:49:58.130 --> 00:50:04.969
But the second administration has been so
focused on trade under your leadership.

00:50:04.969 --> 00:50:14.450
As you look ahead, and if TPP does not go
forward, or even if it does, imagine how any

00:50:14.450 --> 00:50:17.160
administration, do you see that it would just
languish for a full term?

00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:22.119
Or how do you imagine that this might unfold
for a new administration?

00:50:22.119 --> 00:50:26.650
FROMAN: Well, look, I think there is a certain
urgency to getting it done-to getting it done

00:50:26.650 --> 00:50:27.650
this year.

00:50:27.650 --> 00:50:36.380
As you said, we really turned to doing Korea/Colombia/Panama
about three years into the administration,

00:50:36.380 --> 00:50:45.339
and trade was not as prominent a campaign
issue in 2008 as it is in 2016.

00:50:45.339 --> 00:50:50.560
Right now, you've got a president, a majority
leader of the Senate, and a speaker of the

00:50:50.560 --> 00:50:58.410
House who are all pro trade and who've all
been involved in pursuing this agenda.

00:50:58.410 --> 00:51:03.470
And, you know, that could well be different
a year from now.

00:51:03.470 --> 00:51:10.229
And so I think that really does put a focus
on doing everything we can to get this done

00:51:10.229 --> 00:51:11.229
this year.

00:51:11.229 --> 00:51:12.579
JANOW: Absolutely.

00:51:12.579 --> 00:51:15.630
There's a question here.

00:51:15.630 --> 00:51:16.630
Yeah.

00:51:16.630 --> 00:51:17.920
Q: Hi.

00:51:17.920 --> 00:51:20.979
Julien Tizorin from UniCredit.

00:51:20.979 --> 00:51:23.559
Little question regarding the energy sector.

00:51:23.559 --> 00:51:30.239
What does-TPP will provide for the energy
sector in the U.S., especially given that

00:51:30.239 --> 00:51:35.950
now, you know, exporting crude oil is starting
just a little bit-there's been a lot of investment

00:51:35.950 --> 00:51:40.539
in terms of exporting energy and especially
to countries where-you know, which are related

00:51:40.539 --> 00:51:42.619
to the TPP?

00:51:42.619 --> 00:51:49.029
We've also seen the U.S. production capacity
to be very flexible in terms of the collapse

00:51:49.029 --> 00:51:52.210
of the oil price over the past few months.

00:51:52.210 --> 00:51:54.400
What would TPP really allow?

00:51:54.400 --> 00:51:59.359
And how much is it going to drive some of
the U.S. exports, as well?

00:51:59.359 --> 00:52:06.339
FROMAN: TPP itself does not have an energy
chapter or deal with energy issues itself.

00:52:06.339 --> 00:52:13.479
I think under our U.S. law, for example, the
Natural Gas Act, exports of natural gas to

00:52:13.479 --> 00:52:19.901
FTA partners, who also give us access to their
market, is presumed to be in the-in the national

00:52:19.901 --> 00:52:21.710
interest or the public interest.

00:52:21.710 --> 00:52:27.150
And so there's a separate track of licensing
FTA partners for export from non-FTA partners

00:52:27.150 --> 00:52:28.150
for export.

00:52:28.150 --> 00:52:35.039
And the Department of Energy-David knows this
much better than I do-has a process for evaluating

00:52:35.039 --> 00:52:36.499
those licenses accordingly.

00:52:36.499 --> 00:52:42.229
But the trade agreement itself does not actually
deal with energy trade.

00:52:42.229 --> 00:52:44.509
JANOW: Sir.

00:52:44.509 --> 00:52:47.930
Q: Good morning.

00:52:47.930 --> 00:52:52.809
Away from TPP, but to take advantage of your
presence here with us, would you share with

00:52:52.809 --> 00:52:58.239
us your thoughts and insights into the consequences
of Brexit, should the vote go that way-what

00:52:58.239 --> 00:53:01.089
the consequences for the U.K. and for the
EU might be?

00:53:01.089 --> 00:53:04.999
JANOW: And if you can bring that to TPP, that
would be even better.

00:53:04.999 --> 00:53:05.999
(Laughter.)

00:53:05.999 --> 00:53:06.999
FROMAN: Maybe to TTIP.

00:53:06.999 --> 00:53:07.999
(Laughter.)

00:53:07.999 --> 00:53:13.690
I mean, look, this is obviously a decision
that the British people themselves need to

00:53:13.690 --> 00:53:14.690
make.

00:53:14.690 --> 00:53:21.359
But we've made clear-the president made clear
when he was in London that we believe a strong

00:53:21.359 --> 00:53:28.509
U.K. and a strong and unified European Union
is in everybody's interest: in the interest

00:53:28.509 --> 00:53:32.019
of the U.K., in the interest of Europe, and
in our interest.

00:53:32.019 --> 00:53:36.880
We want a strong Europe that is outwardly
focused and capable of being a partner of

00:53:36.880 --> 00:53:42.279
ours in dealing with a whole range of global
issues.

00:53:42.279 --> 00:53:48.519
And that remains-that remains our position,
but it is for the British people to decide.

00:53:48.519 --> 00:53:56.700
And as I sort of alluded to in our-in the
TTIP question earlier, we want to make sure

00:53:56.700 --> 00:54:05.299
that Europe has the ability to engage in a
broad set of issues going forward, and do

00:54:05.299 --> 00:54:09.400
have concerns about how Brexit might play
into that.

00:54:09.400 --> 00:54:18.809
JANOW: So is that-as a point of departure,
imagine we have TPP and TTIP in existence.

00:54:18.809 --> 00:54:23.740
How do you see these rule frameworks interacting
with each other?

00:54:23.740 --> 00:54:28.859
The argument used to be made that these would
escalate into something else over time.

00:54:28.859 --> 00:54:35.039
I don't think we've seen any evidence of that,
but we've seen other ways in which they can

00:54:35.039 --> 00:54:36.039
be reinforcing.

00:54:36.039 --> 00:54:38.009
So how do you see these rule frameworks?

00:54:38.009 --> 00:54:43.539
Because they're not identical, either in the
coverage or in structure.

00:54:43.539 --> 00:54:50.509
FROMAN: They may not be-end up being identical,
but they-our goal is for them both to advance

00:54:50.509 --> 00:54:53.289
the goal of raising standards around the world.

00:54:53.289 --> 00:54:57.380
And if we can do it with TPP and TTIP, that's
two-thirds of the global economy.

00:54:57.380 --> 00:55:02.430
Then you've got the countries who've already
indicated that they want to join TPP, and

00:55:02.430 --> 00:55:07.819
even some who've said they want to join TTIP
once it gets completed, so you can see that

00:55:07.819 --> 00:55:10.099
growing above two-thirds.

00:55:10.099 --> 00:55:16.170
When you've got two-thirds or more of the
global economy saying, here's a good framework

00:55:16.170 --> 00:55:22.219
for the digital economy, for intellectual
property rights, for state-owned enterprise

00:55:22.219 --> 00:55:29.849
disciplines, for science-based rulemaking,
it makes it much easier, then, to multilaterlize

00:55:29.849 --> 00:55:30.859
that.

00:55:30.859 --> 00:55:35.759
Those countries have already gone through
the challenges of getting to those positions

00:55:35.759 --> 00:55:36.759
domestically.

00:55:36.759 --> 00:55:39.089
They brought their stakeholders along.

00:55:39.089 --> 00:55:41.650
And then it makes it easier and easier to
make that a multilateral role.

00:55:41.650 --> 00:55:47.180
And that's ultimately-our objective is-I mean,
the highest and best form of trade liberalization

00:55:47.180 --> 00:55:50.109
is the multilateral system.

00:55:50.109 --> 00:55:56.390
We've seen it stymied over the last 15 years
for a variety of reasons in the Doha Round,

00:55:56.390 --> 00:56:02.119
but we now feel that the WTO is at a moment
in time when it can actually look at new approaches

00:56:02.119 --> 00:56:04.890
to old issues and new issues it can take on.

00:56:04.890 --> 00:56:08.999
And as these mega-regional agreements are
put in place, we think that helps sort of

00:56:08.999 --> 00:56:13.059
create building blocks that ultimately might
help strengthen the multilateral system as

00:56:13.059 --> 00:56:14.059
well.

00:56:14.059 --> 00:56:16.200
JANOW: Mmm hmm.

00:56:16.200 --> 00:56:18.559
Sir, last question.

00:56:18.559 --> 00:56:21.390
Q: Jeff Glueck from Foursquare.

00:56:21.390 --> 00:56:22.390
Hi, Mike.

00:56:22.390 --> 00:56:23.390
Good to see you again.

00:56:23.390 --> 00:56:24.390
FROMAN: Good to see you.

00:56:24.390 --> 00:56:26.420
Q: And a salute, by the way, unrelated, to
your Kids v. Cancer work.

00:56:26.420 --> 00:56:29.700
I'm a big fan of all that you and your wife
have done on that.

00:56:29.700 --> 00:56:35.200
You alluded to the science-based regulation,
and a lot of the criticism of TPP has been

00:56:35.200 --> 00:56:42.170
around usurping sort of democratic and sovereign
and evolving scientific debates about genetically

00:56:42.170 --> 00:56:45.519
modified organisms or synthetic biology and
all that.

00:56:45.519 --> 00:56:50.959
Could you comment on, you know, your answer
to concerns that this would impede things

00:56:50.959 --> 00:56:54.140
like GMO labeling if voters wanted it, things
like that?

00:56:54.140 --> 00:56:55.140
FROMAN: Right.

00:56:55.140 --> 00:56:56.910
There's nothing in there that would impede
any of that.

00:56:56.910 --> 00:57:04.769
I mean, our fundamental focus is-whether it's
in TPP or in TTIP-is to underscore the importance

00:57:04.769 --> 00:57:10.109
of science-based and risk-based decision-making,
which is already established in the WTO.

00:57:10.109 --> 00:57:14.990
These countries have all agreed to that 22
years ago or so, and we want to make sure

00:57:14.990 --> 00:57:17.259
that we're implementing that.

00:57:17.259 --> 00:57:18.569
We trust science.

00:57:18.569 --> 00:57:24.589
Whether they're European scientists, or Japanese
scientists, or wherever the science is done,

00:57:24.589 --> 00:57:30.680
we trust science because we think it's a very
sound basis on which to make these decisions.

00:57:30.680 --> 00:57:35.539
Our challenge has oftentimes been in countries
where the scientists say something is safe,

00:57:35.539 --> 00:57:41.459
but the politicians then vote on safety and
decide whether they think it's safe or not.

00:57:41.459 --> 00:57:47.569
And that, I think, is challenge to the rules-based
system that we all agreed to in the WTO.

00:57:47.569 --> 00:57:52.690
And so, as-you know, with-this an issue, certainly
a significant issue in TTIP, and you allude

00:57:52.690 --> 00:57:55.400
to it in TPP as well.

00:57:55.400 --> 00:58:00.510
We're not trying to force anybody to eat anything,
but we do think the decisions about what is

00:58:00.510 --> 00:58:05.900
safe ought to made by science, not by politics
and not by protectionism.

00:58:05.900 --> 00:58:07.460
And that's our position in both agreements.

00:58:07.460 --> 00:58:14.170
JANOW: And that's been true, as you say, since
pre-Uruguay Round, so.

00:58:14.170 --> 00:58:17.660
Well, with that, it's wonderful to have you
here.

00:58:17.660 --> 00:58:19.230
Please come back.

00:58:19.230 --> 00:58:21.130
Please join me in thanking Ambassador Froman.

00:58:21.130 --> 00:58:22.130
(Applause.)

00:58:22.130 --> 00:58:22.131
(END)

